{"causes": ["The decisive Uí Néill victory at the Battle of Mag Rath in 637 CE eliminated major rivals and paved the way for Cellach's ascendancy.", "The established tradition of rotating kingship among the Uí Néill branches created a path for Cellach's Cenél Conaill lineage to claim the High Kingship.", "The death of his cousin Domnall mac Áedo in 642 CE created a power vacuum that Cellach and his brother Conall Cóel were positioned to fill."], "effects": ["Cellach's co-reign with Conall Cóel solidified the Cenél Conaill's claim to the High Kingship for a generation.", "His rule contributed to the ongoing political stability and territorial integrity of the Uí Néill confederation in early medieval Ireland.", "The succession after Cellach's death led to increasing competition between the Northern and Southern Uí Néill branches for the High Kingship."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "domnall-mac-aedo", "sourceName": "Domnall mac Áedo", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "cellach-mac-mele-coba", "targetName": "Cellach mac Máele Coba", "context": "Cellach mac Máele Coba succeeded his cousin Domnall mac Áedo as High King of Ireland in 642 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "cellach-mac-mele-coba", "sourceName": "Cellach mac Máele Coba", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "conall-coel", "targetName": "Conall Cóel", "context": "Cellach mac Máele Coba co-reigned as High King of Ireland with his brother Conall Cóel from 642 CE until 658 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-mag-rath", "sourceName": "Battle of Mag Rath", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cellach-mac-mele-coba", "targetName": "Cellach mac Máele Coba", "context": "The decisive Uí Néill victory at the Battle of Mag Rath in 637 CE significantly strengthened the position of Cellach's lineage, enabling his later ascension to the High Kingship."}, {"sourceSlug": "cellach-mac-mele-coba", "sourceName": "Cellach mac Máele Coba", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "hill-of-tara-ireland", "targetName": "Hill of Tara, Ireland", "context": "Cellach mac Máele Coba's reign as High King of Ireland was symbolically centered around the ancient royal site of the Hill of Tara."}, {"sourceSlug": "cellach-mac-mele-coba", "sourceName": "Cellach mac Máele Coba", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "ui-neill-dynasty", "targetName": "Uí Néill Dynasty", "context": "Cellach mac Máele Coba was a prominent member of the Uí Néill dynasty, specifically the Cenél Conaill branch, during its period of dominant influence in Ireland."}], "places": [{"name": "Hill of Tara, Ireland", "role": "Traditional Seat of High Kingship"}, {"name": "Donegal, Ireland", "role": "Homeland of Cenél Conaill"}, {"name": "Ulster, Ireland", "role": "Region of Ancestral Power"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cellach mac Máele Coba", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "cellach-mac-máele-coba"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1154349", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellach_mac_M%C3%A1ele_Coba"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Cellach mac Máele Coba is remembered as a landmark person of the Classical era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries; Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of political thought and imperial studies.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Cellach mac Máele Coba's co-reign as High King of Ireland from 642-658 CE was crucial in maintaining the Uí Néill's political supremacy following the Battle of Mag Rath, solidifying a lineage that would continue to shape Irish politics for centuries. His rule exemplifies the complex dynastic struggles and power-sharing arrangements of early medieval Gaelic society.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-04T07:19:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28698632815", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cellach mac Máele Coba was a economist or social scientist of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of monarchs & rulers.", "newValue": "Cellach mac Máele Coba, a pivotal figure in early medieval Irish history, reigned as King of Cenél Conaill from approximately 654 CE and, crucially, as High King of Ireland (Ard Rí Éirenn) from 642 CE until his death in 658 CE. His co-reign with his brother Conall Cóel marked a period of significant political consolidation and military engagement for the Uí Néill dynasty, particularly the northern branch. Succeeding his cousin Domnall mac Áedo, Cellach navigated the complex and often violent landscape of Gaelic kingship, asserting authority over rival provincial kings and maintaining the supremacy of the Uí Néill over much of the island.

The core of Cellach's reign was defined by the aftermath of the Battle of Mag Rath (Moira) in 637 CE, a decisive victory for Domnall mac Áedo over the Ulaid and Dál Riata, which greatly weakened the northern rivals of the Uí Néill. Upon Domnall's death in 642 CE, Cellach and Conall Cóel ascended to the High Kingship, a rare instance of co-rulership for this office. Their collective authority was challenged by the rise of the Southern Uí Néill, particularly the Clann Cholmáin and Síl nÁedo Sláine, leading to internal dynastic struggles that would characterize much of the subsequent century. The brothers are recorded in the annals as having ruled from the legendary Hill of Tara, symbolizing their traditional suzerainty over the island.

Cellach's legacy lies in his contribution to the enduring political dominance of the Uí Néill in Ireland, solidifying the gains made by his predecessors and setting the stage for future generations of High Kings from his lineage. His co-reign with Conall Cóel illustrates a unique adaptive strategy within the Uí Néill succession patterns, temporarily bridging potential internecine conflicts. Though his rule was not without challenges, it represented a continuation of a powerful political tradition, ensuring the Cenél Conaill's influence persisted for decades."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-04T07:19:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28698632815", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The decisive Uí Néill victory at the Battle of Mag Rath in 637 CE eliminated major rivals and paved the way for Cellach's ascendancy.", "The established tradition of rotating kingship among the Uí Néill branches created a path for Cellach's Cenél Conaill lineage to claim the High Kingship.", "The death of his cousin Domnall mac Áedo in 642 CE created a power vacuum that Cellach and his brother Conall Cóel were positioned to fill."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-04T07:19:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28698632815", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "658 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "658 CE"}], "newValue": ["Cellach's co-reign with Conall Cóel solidified the Cenél Conaill's claim to the High Kingship for a generation.", "His rule contributed to the ongoing political stability and territorial in